/* Librarian notes for the Room of the Day, keyed by the title used in data/daily-rooms.js. A book that
   already has a note elsewhere in the library keeps it; these fill in the rest (see daily-room.js). */
window.ATHENAEUM_DAILY_NOTES={
  // A Rainy Evening
  'The Woman in White':'It begins with a drawing master walking home by moonlight and a woman dressed all in white stepping out of the dark. Collins tells it through a relay of witnesses, each one knowing a little less than they think. Sensation fiction at its most elegant.',
  'Northanger Abbey':'Catherine Morland has read too many gothic novels and expects every old abbey to hide a crime. Austen teases her gently and the genre much less gently. The joke is affectionate: this is a book about how much we love being frightened.',
  'The Fall of the House of Usher':'A crumbling house, a brother too sensitive to bear the light, a sister who should not be walking. Poe packs a whole gothic novel into a few pages. Notice the crack running down the front of the house, and read it on a stormy night.',
  'The Mystery of Edwin Drood':'Dickens died halfway through writing it, and nobody knows how it ends. A young man vanishes on Christmas Eve; his uncle, a choirmaster with an opium habit, grieves suspiciously well. Readers have been proposing solutions for a hundred and fifty years.',
  'Uncle Silas':'An heiress is sent to live with her mysterious uncle in a decaying house in Derbyshire, where the doors lock from the outside. Le Fanu builds dread slowly and patiently, and the governess is among the most unsettling characters in Victorian fiction.',
  'The Lodger':'A quiet gentleman takes the Buntings’ upstairs rooms just as a killer begins stalking London’s fog. Mrs Bunting starts to wonder. Belloc Lowndes wrote it from the Ripper murders, and Hitchcock filmed it. The suspense lives almost entirely in a landlady’s head.',
  'The Secret Agent':'A shabby shop in Soho, a lazy double agent, and a bomb plot that goes horribly wrong. Conrad writes about terrorism with bleak irony and not a scrap of glamour. London here is permanently damp, dim and indifferent.',
  // Around the World
  'Following the Equator':'Twain’s lecture tour around the British Empire in the 1890s: Australia, India, South Africa. He was travelling to pay off his debts, and the jokes are sharper and sadder than in his earlier travel books. Each chapter opens with a line from Pudd’nhead Wilson’s New Calendar.',
  'The Voyage of the Beagle':'Five years at sea as a young naturalist, collecting beetles, fossils and finches, and quietly beginning to think the thoughts that became the Origin of Species. Darwin is a wonderful travel writer: curious, seasick and delighted by almost everything.',
  'Sailing Alone Around the World':'Between 1895 and 1898 Joshua Slocum sailed a rebuilt oyster sloop around the world single-handed, the first person to do so. He scattered tacks on his deck to deter night-time boarders and hallucinated a friendly pilot from Columbus’s crew. A calm, dry, marvellous book.',
  'Eothen':'A young Englishman rides through the Ottoman lands in the 1830s and writes about it with a wit that still sparkles. Kinglake made the travel book personal: less about ruins, more about the traveller’s own impressions, moods and mishaps.',
  'Travels with a Donkey in the Cevennes':'Stevenson walks the mountains of southern France with Modestine, a small and stubborn donkey whom he first beats and then misses terribly. He sleeps outdoors under pine trees and thinks about Protestant rebels. A short, lovely book.',
  'The Travels of Marco Polo':'Dictated in a Genoese prison around 1298, it described the court of Kublai Khan, paper money and cities larger than any in Europe. Readers called it a book of marvels and doubted half of it. Columbus carried a copy.',
  // Mind Your Manners
  'Etiquette':'Emily Post’s 1922 guide made her the arbiter of American manners for decades. It is more generous than its reputation: she thought good manners were about consideration, not snobbery. The chapters on weddings and chaperones are a time capsule.',
  'Manners and Social Usages':'Mrs Sherwood advised the new rich of Gilded Age New York on how to behave as though they had always been rich. Calling cards, afternoon teas, mourning dress: every rule is a small window onto a society anxious to be taken seriously.',
  'The Book of the Courtier':'Castiglione imagines the court of Urbino in 1507 debating what makes the perfect courtier. His answer, sprezzatura, the art of making difficult things look effortless, shaped European manners for three centuries.',
  'Enquire Within Upon Everything':'A Victorian household oracle: how to remove ink stains, cure a cold, write a letter of condolence or play whist. It sold over a million copies, and Tim Berners-Lee named an early precursor of the World Wide Web after it.',
  "The Ladies' Book of Etiquette, and Manual of Politeness":'Florence Hartley’s 1860 guide to letters, visits, dress and conversation for American women. Between the rules about gloves you can read a whole life of constant, careful self-presentation.',
  "The Gentlemen's Book of Etiquette and Manual of Politeness":'The male counterpart: how to bow, how to carve, how to be introduced, and when a gentleman may smoke. The advice on conversation (listen more than you talk) remains excellent.',
  'Our Deportment':'An 1880s American compendium of manners for every occasion from christenings to funerals. It assumes that correct behaviour can be learned from a book, which is both touching and exactly why it sold.',
  // Harvest Home
  'Adam Bede':'George Eliot’s first novel, set among the farms and dairies of a Midlands village in 1799. A carpenter loves a dairymaid who loves the squire’s grandson. Eliot writes the harvest supper and the dairy with such exactness you can smell the butter.',
  'Rural Rides':'William Cobbett rode through the English countryside in the 1820s, raging at landlords, bankers and the misery of farm labourers. It is part travel book, part political pamphlet, and full of the look of hedges, soils and crops.',
  'The Georgics':'Virgil’s poem on farming: ploughing, vines, cattle and bees. It was written as Rome came out of civil war, and every practical instruction carries the hope of peace. The fourth book, on the beekeeper Aristaeus, is its most beautiful.',
  "The Farmer's Boy":'Robert Bloomfield, a shoemaker who had worked on a Suffolk farm as a boy, wrote this poem of the farming year in a London garret. Published in 1800, it sold tens of thousands of copies. Spring sowing, summer harvest, autumn gleaning, winter fields.',
  'The Woodlanders':'Hardy’s novel of a Dorset woodland village, where people plant trees, make cider and quietly break each other’s hearts. Giles Winterborne, who works with apples and orchards, is one of his most loyal and unlucky men.',
  'My Antonia':'Willa Cather’s novel of Nebraska homesteaders and a Bohemian girl who grows up in the fields. The harvest scenes are golden and hard-won. Jim Burden remembers the prairie as a place where you felt you were at the edge of the world.',
  // Born at Michaelmas
  'North and South':'Margaret Hale moves from a gentle southern village to a smoky northern mill town and clashes with a mill owner, John Thornton. Gaskell writes about strikes, pride and class with real fairness to both sides, and it is also a very good love story.',
  'Mary Barton':'Gaskell’s first novel, about Manchester mill workers in the hungry 1840s. It made middle-class readers look at poverty they preferred not to see, and was attacked as unfair to the masters. Its anger is still alive.',
  'Wives and Daughters':'A doctor’s daughter, Molly Gibson, gains a stepmother and a dazzling stepsister. Gaskell’s last novel is warm, funny and wise about families; she died before finishing the final chapter, and the editor’s closing note is heartbreaking.',
  'Ruth':'A young seamstress is seduced and abandoned, then taken in by a dissenting minister who hides her past. Gaskell’s 1853 novel was so sympathetic to a “fallen woman” that some readers burned it. It is braver than its reputation.',
  'Cousin Phillis':'A short, perfect novella about a minister’s daughter on a farm, her cousin, and the arrival of the railway engineer who changes everything. The countryside and the coming of the modern world meet in a very small space.',
  "Sylvia's Lovers":'Set in a Yorkshire whaling town in the 1790s, when press gangs seized men for the navy. Sylvia must choose between a dashing harpooner and a faithful cousin. Gaskell considered it her saddest book.',
  'Exemplary Novels':'Twelve short novels by Cervantes, written alongside Don Quixote: gypsies, thieves, a man who believes he is made of glass, and two talking dogs. They show his range when he was not busy with his knight.',
  'The Wanderings of Persiles and Sigismunda':'Cervantes’s last book, finished days before his death in 1616. It is a sprawling romance of lovers travelling from the frozen north to Rome in disguise. He thought it would be his masterpiece; history chose the knight instead.',
  // One Sitting
  'Bartleby, the Scrivener':'A Wall Street copyist begins to answer every request with “I would prefer not to.” Melville’s strange little story is funny, then unsettling, then very sad. Nobody agrees what Bartleby means, which is part of why he lasts.',
  'The Gift of the Magi':'A poor young couple, one dollar and eighty-seven cents, and two Christmas presents bought at great sacrifice. O. Henry’s twist ending is famous, but the tenderness is what makes it last.',
  "The Monkey's Paw":'A mummified paw grants three wishes, and every wish comes true in the worst possible way. W. W. Jacobs wrote mostly comic stories about sailors; this one has frightened readers since 1902. Listen for the knocking.',
  'An Occurrence at Owl Creek Bridge':'A man is about to be hanged from a railway bridge during the American Civil War. The rope breaks, or does it? Bierce’s story is barely a dozen pages and has one of the most famous endings in American fiction.',
  'Rip Van Winkle':'A lazy, likeable Dutch villager wanders into the Catskill Mountains, drinks with some strange bowling men, and sleeps for twenty years. He comes home to find a revolution has happened without him.',
  'The Luck of Roaring Camp':'A baby is born in a rough California mining camp, and the miners, gamblers and outcasts decide to raise him. Bret Harte’s 1868 story made him famous overnight. Sentimental, yes, and also genuinely moving.',
  // The Brontës
  'Agnes Grey':'Anne Brontë drew on her own years as a governess for this plain, honest novel about a clergyman’s daughter working for unkind families. It lacks the storms of her sisters’ books and has a quiet courage of its own.',
  'The Tenant of Wildfell Hall':'A mysterious widow moves into a ruined hall with her young son, and the village gossips. Anne Brontë’s second novel is a fierce account of an abusive marriage and a woman who leaves it. It shocked readers in 1848.',
  'Villette':'Lucy Snowe teaches in a girls’ school in a Belgian city and tells us only what she chooses to. Charlotte Brontë’s last novel is her most daring: lonely, strange, with a ghostly nun and an ending that refuses to settle.',
  'Shirley':'Set among Yorkshire mill owners and machine-breakers in 1811–12. Charlotte Brontë wrote it while her brother and both sisters died, and her grief shows. Shirley Keeldar was partly modelled on Emily.',
  'The Professor':'Charlotte Brontë’s first novel, rejected by publishers and printed only after her death. A young Englishman teaches in Brussels and falls in love with a pupil. Many of the ideas behind Villette appear here in rougher form.',
  'Poems by Currer, Ellis, and Acton Bell':'The sisters’ first book, printed at their own expense in 1846. It sold two copies. Emily’s poems in it, including “Remembrance” (“Cold in the earth”), are among the strongest verse of the century.',
  'The Life of Charlotte Bronte':'Gaskell’s biography of her friend, published two years after Charlotte’s death. It created the legend of the lonely parsonage on the moors. Parts were so frank that threatened lawsuits forced revisions.',
  // Firsts of Fiction
  'The Castle of Otranto':'A giant helmet falls from the sky and crushes a prince on his wedding day. Horace Walpole’s 1764 novel invented the gothic: haunted castles, secret passages, tyrannical lords and fainting heroines. It is absurd and influential in equal measure.',
  'Phantastes':'A young man wakes to find his bedroom turning into a forest and walks into Fairy Land. George MacDonald’s 1858 dream-romance is often called the first fantasy novel for adults. C. S. Lewis said reading it baptised his imagination.',
  'The Moonstone':'A sacred Indian diamond vanishes from a Yorkshire country house on a birthday night. T. S. Eliot called it the first, longest and best of modern English detective novels. It has a detective who grows roses and a butler who quotes Robinson Crusoe.',
  'Pamela':'A fifteen-year-old servant resists her master’s advances and writes about it in letters home. Richardson’s 1740 novel was a sensation and helped create the modern novel; it was also mocked mercilessly by Fielding.',
  'Utopia':'Thomas More described an island with no private property, where gold is used for chamber pots. His 1516 book gave the world the word utopia, which means both “good place” and “no place”. Nobody is sure how seriously he meant it.',
  // Letters and Diaries
  'The Diary of Samuel Pepys':'From 1660 to 1669 a naval administrator kept a private diary in shorthand: plague, the Great Fire, the King’s return, theatre, wine and infidelities. Pepys buried his parmesan cheese in the garden to save it from the fire.',
  'Journals of Dorothy Wordsworth':'Dorothy walked the Lake District with her brother William and noted everything: weather, wildflowers, beggars on the road. Her description of daffodils by Ullswater became his most famous poem. Hers is arguably better.',
  'Letters of a Woman Homesteader':'In 1909 a widowed washerwoman moved to Wyoming with her small daughter to claim land. Elinore Pruitt Stewart’s letters to her former employer are funny, brave and full of cowboys, blizzards and neighbours.',
  'Letters of Abelard and Heloise':'A famous scholar and his brilliant pupil fell in love in twelfth-century Paris; her family took a terrible revenge, and both entered monasteries. Their later letters argue about love, faith and memory with astonishing honesty.',
  'Letters to His Son':'Lord Chesterfield wrote to his illegitimate son for decades, advising him how to succeed in society. The letters are witty, worldly and occasionally cynical. Samuel Johnson disliked them intensely.',
  'Letters of Madame de Sévigné':'A seventeenth-century French aristocrat wrote more than a thousand letters to her daughter. Court gossip, country life and a mother’s longing are all here, in a style so lively that the letters are counted as literature.',
  'Evelina':'A young woman enters London society and writes home about her mortifications at balls, theatres and pleasure gardens. Fanny Burney’s 1778 novel in letters was a great success and a clear influence on Jane Austen.',
  'Lady Susan':'Austen’s short early novel in letters, about a beautiful, clever and thoroughly wicked widow manipulating everyone around her. Austen never published it. Lady Susan is the most enjoyable villain she wrote.',
  // The Sea
  'Captains Courageous':'A spoiled American boy falls off a liner and is rescued by a Grand Banks fishing schooner, whose crew refuse to take him home until the season ends. Kipling’s only American novel is a story of growing up among cod, fog and hard work.',
  'Typhoon':'Captain MacWhirr is stolid, literal and entirely without imagination, and he sails his steamer straight into a typhoon. Conrad’s novella is a storm told from inside, and a strange tribute to dull courage.',
  'Mr. Midshipman Easy':'A wealthy young philosopher joins the navy believing all men are equal and soon learns otherwise. Captain Marryat served under Lord Cochrane and wrote sea stories full of real naval life and schoolboy comedy.',
  'The Cruise of the Cachalot':'Frank Bullen shipped aboard a New Bedford whaler as a young man, and this 1898 account of the voyage is one of the best first-hand pictures of the whaling trade. Kipling admired it enormously.',
  // The Victorian Kitchen
  'The Book of Household Management':'Isabella Beeton was twenty-five when this enormous guide was published in 1861, covering recipes, servants, medicine and law. Many of the recipes were borrowed from other cooks, but the organisation was all her own. She died at twenty-eight.',
  'Modern Cookery for Private Families':'Eliza Acton’s 1845 cookbook was the first to list ingredients separately and give exact quantities and times. She was a poet before she was a cook, and her writing is precise and graceful. Mrs Beeton borrowed freely from her.',
  'A New System of Domestic Cookery':'Maria Rundell’s 1806 book was the best-selling cookbook of its day in Britain and America. It is practical, thrifty and full of advice for the young housekeeper. She handed the copyright to her publisher, John Murray, as a gift.',
  'The Physiology of Taste':'A French magistrate’s 1825 meditations on eating: digestion, dreams, obesity, the pleasures of the table. Brillat-Savarin coined “Tell me what you eat and I will tell you what you are.” It is witty, digressive and delicious.',
  'The Boston Cooking-School Cook Book':'Fannie Farmer’s 1896 cookbook insisted on standard level measurements, which changed American home cooking. It was the most used cookbook in the country for decades.',
  'The Forme of Cury':'A roll of recipes compiled by the master cooks of Richard II around 1390, edited in 1780 by Samuel Pegge. Blancmange, spiced wine and many sauces thickened with breadcrumbs: a glimpse of the medieval royal kitchen.',
  'The Italian Cook Book':'Published in New York in 1919 to teach Americans the art of Italian cooking. Pasta, risotto and minestrone arrive in American kitchens, explained patiently for readers who had never met them.',
  // Autumn Gathers
  'The Private Papers of Henry Ryecroft':'A tired writer inherits enough money to retire to a Devon cottage and spends a year reading, walking and remembering hardship. Gissing arranged it by season, and it is one of the great books of contentment.',
  'Excursions':'A collection of Thoreau’s nature essays, published after his death, including “Autumnal Tints”, his celebration of the scarlet oaks and red maples of New England in the fall. He urges us to look properly at a single leaf.',
  'Wild Life in a Southern County':'Richard Jefferies walks the Wiltshire downs and writes about everything he sees: hares, rooks, farmers, poachers, the weather. His 1879 book made natural-history writing into literature.',
  'A Week on the Concord and Merrimack Rivers':'Thoreau’s first book recalls a boat trip he took with his brother John, who later died. It wanders from river scenes into essays on friendship, poetry and religion. It sold so badly that he stored most of the print run in his attic.',
  'Our Village':'Mary Russell Mitford’s sketches of life in a Berkshire village in the 1820s: cricket matches, walks, neighbours and seasons. She wrote them to support her father, who gambled away the family money. They are gentle, observant and funny.',
  'The Return of the Native':'Egdon Heath is so vividly described that it becomes the book’s main character. The novel opens with bonfires lit on 5 November and follows Eustacia Vye, who longs to escape the heath. Hardy at his most atmospheric.',
  // Poe's Last Day, Riley's First
  'The Raven':'A grieving student, a midnight visitor tapping at the chamber door, and a single word: nevermore. Poe’s 1845 poem made him famous overnight, though it earned him only a few dollars. Read it aloud, quietly, near midnight.',
  'The Cask of Amontillado':'During carnival, Montresor lures his rival into the catacombs with the promise of a rare sherry. Poe tells a perfect revenge from the murderer’s point of view, calmly, fifty years later. The last line is chilling.',
  'Eureka: A Prose Poem':'Poe’s last major work, published in 1848, tries to explain the origin and fate of the universe. It imagines the cosmos expanding from a single particle and collapsing back again. Poe thought it his masterpiece; scientists still find it uncanny.',
  'Afterwhiles':'A collection of Riley’s poems from 1887, mixing dialect verse with more formal lyrics. The mood is gentle and backward-looking, remembering childhood, old friends and summers that seemed endless.',
  // Fables and Parables
  "Aesop's Fables":'The tortoise and the hare, the fox and the grapes, the boy who cried wolf. Nobody knows if Aesop existed, but these tiny stories have taught morals for twenty-five centuries. Each one takes less than a minute and lasts a lifetime.',
  'The Fables of La Fontaine':'In seventeenth-century France, Jean de La Fontaine retold Aesop and other fables in elegant, ironic verse. The animals behave like courtiers at Versailles. Every French child still learns “The Crow and the Fox”.',
  'Fables for the Frivolous':'Guy Wetmore Carryl retold old fables in comic American verse in 1898, twisting the morals into jokes. The rhymes are dazzlingly clever. It is the fable as a party piece.',
  'The Prophet':'Kahlil Gibran’s 1923 book of poetic essays: a prophet leaving a city is asked about love, marriage, children, work and death. It has sold millions of copies and is read at a great many weddings.',
  "Hans Andersen's Fairy Tales":'The Little Mermaid, The Ugly Duckling, The Emperor’s New Clothes, The Snow Queen. Andersen invented many of his tales rather than collecting them, and they carry his own sadness and hope. They are not as gentle as films make them.',
  'Fables in Slang':'George Ade’s 1899 fables were written in the slang of Chicago offices and saloons, with a moral at the end of each. They were a huge success. The humour is modern, sharp and very American.',
  'Fantastic Fables':'Ambrose Bierce’s 1899 fables are short, savage and very funny. Politicians, lawyers and clergymen appear as themselves or as animals, and none of them come off well.',
  'The Madman: His Parables and Poems':'Gibran’s first book in English, from 1918. A man loses his masks and is called mad, and finds himself free. The parables are brief and strange, darker than The Prophet.',
  // The Transcendentalists
  'Essays — First Series':'Emerson’s 1841 collection includes “Self-Reliance”, with its famous advice to trust yourself and its warning that “a foolish consistency is the hobgoblin of little minds”. These essays shaped American independence of thought.',
  'Nature':'Emerson’s 1836 essay was the manifesto of Transcendentalism: nature as a living symbol of spirit, and each person able to meet the divine directly. It includes the image of becoming “a transparent eyeball”, which is odder and more beautiful than it sounds.',
  'Essays — Second Series':'Emerson’s 1844 essays, including “Experience”, written after the death of his young son. It is his most personal and troubled essay. Also here: “The Poet” and “Nature”, a different essay from the famous book.',
  'Concord Days':'Bronson Alcott, Louisa May Alcott’s father, was a visionary teacher and talker, and a hopeless provider. These 1872 notes and reflections recall Concord’s great neighbours: Emerson, Thoreau and Hawthorne.',
  'The Maine Woods':'Thoreau’s three trips into the Maine wilderness, including a climb of Mount Katahdin, where he found nature raw and not at all welcoming. “Contact! Contact! Who are we? Where are we?” he cries on the bare summit.',
  'Representative Men':'Emerson’s lectures on six great men: Plato, Swedenborg, Montaigne, Shakespeare, Napoleon and Goethe. Each stands for a kind of human possibility. The essay on Montaigne, the sceptic, is the most personal.',
  // First Novels
  'The Pickwick Papers':'The book that made Dickens famous at twenty-four. Mr Pickwick and his friends travel England getting into scrapes, and when Sam Weller the cockney bootblack appears in the fourth instalment, sales soared. Pure comic joy.',
  "Almayer's Folly":'Conrad’s first novel, published in 1895, about a Dutch trader dreaming of gold in a Borneo river settlement. Conrad wrote it in English, his third language, while still working at sea.',
  'This Side of Paradise':'Fitzgerald’s 1920 debut, about a Princeton student named Amory Blaine, made him a celebrity at twenty-three. It defined the Jazz Age before the phrase existed. It is messy, brilliant and very young.',
  'The Voyage Out':'Virginia Woolf’s first novel, from 1915. Rachel Vinrace sails to South America, discovers the world and falls in love. It is more traditional than her later books, but the sea and the strangeness are already hers.',
  'Desperate Remedies':'Thomas Hardy’s first published novel, a sensation story from 1871 with a secret marriage, a fire and a murder. It is melodramatic, and you can already see the Dorset landscapes and the pull of fate that became his signature.',
  'Waverley':'Walter Scott’s 1814 novel about a young Englishman caught up in the Jacobite rising of 1745 invented the historical novel. Scott published it anonymously and was known for years as “the Author of Waverley”.',
  'Typee':'Melville’s first book, from 1846, based on his own weeks among the people of a valley in the Marquesas after deserting a whaler. It was a bestseller, and for years he was famous as “the man who lived among cannibals”.',
  // Lost Worlds
  'The Land That Time Forgot':'A U-boat, a lost island near Antarctica, and dinosaurs. Burroughs published it in 1918 as pulp adventure, and underneath the peril it hides an odd theory of evolution. It moves at a gallop.',
  "At the Earth's Core":'An inventor’s mechanical mole digs five hundred miles down and breaks into Pellucidar, a world inside the hollow Earth lit by a sun that never sets. Burroughs at full speed: monsters, slaves and a telepathic reptile race.',
  'The Moon Pool':'An expedition in the South Pacific finds a gateway to an underground world guarded by a shining, soul-stealing presence. A. Merritt’s 1919 novel influenced a whole generation of fantasy and weird fiction writers.',
  'Atlantida':'Two French officers in the Sahara stumble into the hidden kingdom of Antinea, the last queen of Atlantis, who keeps her dead lovers in a gallery. Pierre Benoit’s 1919 novel was a huge success in France.',
  // Across the Atlantic
  'A Short Account of the Destruction of the Indies':'A Spanish friar who had once held land and enslaved people himself wrote this 1552 account of the violence of the conquest, to persuade the king to stop it. It is harrowing and was hugely influential.',
  'The Interesting Narrative of the Life of Olaudah Equiano':'Equiano was kidnapped as a child, enslaved, and eventually bought his own freedom. His 1789 autobiography was a bestseller and a powerful weapon in the campaign against the slave trade. He crossed the Atlantic many times, and saw both sides of it.',
  'Letters from an American Farmer':'A French-born farmer in New York asked in 1782, “What then is the American, this new man?” Crèvecoeur’s letters praise the freedom of the new country and then, near the end, turn dark with slavery and war.',
  'American Notes':'Dickens toured America in 1842 and wrote about prisons, factories, spitting and slavery. Americans were furious. His admiration and irritation are both honest, and his account of solitary confinement is unforgettable.',
  'Domestic Manners of the Americans':'Frances Trollope, mother of Anthony, lived in Cincinnati for several years and wrote this sharp, snobbish, funny book in 1832. Americans hated it. She particularly disliked tobacco chewing.',
  'The Last of the Mohicans':'The frontier in 1757, during the French and Indian War: Hawkeye, Chingachgook and Uncas guide two sisters through the forests. Cooper’s adventure shaped how the world imagined early America, for better and worse.',
  'Democracy in America':'A young French aristocrat came to study American prisons in 1831 and wrote the most famous book about American democracy. Tocqueville saw its energy and its dangers, including the tyranny of the majority.',
  'O Pioneers!':'Alexandra Bergson, a Swedish immigrant’s daughter, takes over her father’s farm on the Nebraska prairie and makes it flourish. Willa Cather’s 1913 novel is about the land as much as the people who work it.',
  // Science That Was Wrong
  'Memoirs of Extraordinary Popular Delusions and the Madness of Crowds':'Charles Mackay’s 1841 catalogue of human folly: tulip mania, the South Sea Bubble, alchemists, witch hunts and fortune tellers. It is a history of manias that feels uncomfortably fresh in every century.',
  'Vestiges of the Natural History of Creation':'Published anonymously in 1844, this bestseller argued that everything in nature had developed over time. It got many details wrong and caused a scandal. The author was the Edinburgh publisher Robert Chambers, and it prepared readers for Darwin.',
  "Symmes's Theory of Concentric Spheres":'In 1818 John Cleves Symmes announced that the Earth was hollow, with openings at the poles, and asked for volunteers to explore it. James McBride’s 1826 book set out the theory. It inspired a great deal of fiction, including Poe.',
  'Omphalos':'Philip Henry Gosse, a fine naturalist, argued in 1857 that God created the world with fossils already in the rocks and Adam with a navel. The book pleased neither scientists nor clergy. His son Edmund wrote about it movingly in Father and Son.',
  'The Book of the Damned':'Charles Fort collected reports of frogs falling from the sky, strange lights and other things science had “damned” by ignoring them. His 1919 book is weird, funny and gave us the word Fortean.',
  'Mars':'The astronomer Percival Lowell was convinced he saw canals on Mars, built by an intelligent civilisation to carry water from the poles. His 1895 book made the case eloquently. The canals were an optical illusion.',
  // The Fireside Season
  'The Cricket on the Hearth':'One of Dickens’s Christmas books: a carrier, his young wife and a cricket that chirps on their hearth as a household spirit. It is sentimental and cosy, and was hugely popular on the stage.',
  'Snow-Bound':'Whittier remembers a New England snowstorm from his childhood, when his family were shut in for days by the fire, telling stories. Published in 1866, after the Civil War, it looks back to a lost world of warmth.',
  'Tales of a Wayside Inn':'Longfellow gathers a group of friends by the fire at a real Massachusetts inn and has each tell a story. The first is “Paul Revere’s Ride”. The storytellers are based on his own friends.',
  'The Canterbury Tales':'Thirty pilgrims on the road to Canterbury agree to tell stories for a free supper. Chaucer’s tales range from bawdy to holy, and the pilgrims themselves are the best characters in medieval literature.',
  "The Arabian Nights' Entertainments":'Scheherazade tells the king a story each night, always stopping at the most exciting moment, to save her life. Sinbad, Aladdin and Ali Baba all arrived in Europe through this collection.',
  'The Decameron':'In 1348, ten young Florentines flee the plague to a country villa and tell a hundred stories over ten days. Boccaccio’s tales are witty, bawdy and tender, and Chaucer and Shakespeare both borrowed from them.',
  'The Autocrat of the Breakfast-Table':'Oliver Wendell Holmes, a doctor and poet, imagines himself talking to fellow boarders at breakfast in a Boston boarding house. The conversation is witty and wandering, and full of good lines.',
  // Wodehouse, Nietzsche and Virgil
  'My Man Jeeves':'The first Jeeves and Wooster collection, from 1919. Bertie gets into trouble, Jeeves glides in to get him out. Several stories feature a different pair, Reggie Pepper and his friends, but the formula is already golden.',
  'Something New':'The first Blandings Castle novel, from 1915. Lord Emsworth absent-mindedly pockets a precious scarab, and a young man takes a job as a valet to steal it back. The castle, the pigs and the chaos begin here.',
  'Psmith in the City':'Psmith, the monocled, impeccably polite hero who calls everyone “Comrade”, goes to work in a London bank. Wodehouse drew on his own miserable years as a bank clerk.',
  'Piccadilly Jim':'A young American in London, with a scandalous reputation, must pretend to be someone else to win a girl who despises the real him. Wodehouse’s 1917 comedy of mistaken identity moves between London and New York.',
  'A Damsel in Distress':'An American composer falls for an English aristocrat’s daughter who jumps into his taxi. Wodehouse’s 1919 novel has castles, butlers and a sweepstake among the servants. George Gershwin later wrote the film songs.',
  'The Aeneid':'Aeneas flees burning Troy and wanders to Italy to found the line of Rome. Virgil worked on it for eleven years and asked for it to be burned when he died. Augustus refused. This is Dryden’s great 1697 translation.',
  'The Joyful Wisdom':'Also known as The Gay Science, from 1882. It is here that Nietzsche’s madman announces that “God is dead” and asks what we will do now. It is also, surprisingly, a book of cheerfulness and poems.',
  // Wilde in Short
  'The Happy Prince, and Other Tales':'Wilde’s fairy tales, written for his young sons. A golden statue gives away his jewels to the poor with the help of a swallow. They are beautiful, witty and often heartbreaking.',
  'A House of Pomegranates':'Wilde’s second book of fairy tales, darker and more ornate than the first. The Star-Child and The Fisherman and His Soul are strange, lovely stories about vanity, cruelty and love.',
  "Lord Arthur Savile's Crime; The Portrait of Mr. W. H., and Other Stories":'A palm reader tells Lord Arthur he will commit a murder, so he dutifully sets about getting it over with before his wedding. The title story is one of Wilde’s funniest. The others include The Canterville Ghost.',
  'The Importance of Being Earnest':'Two young men invent imaginary people to escape their obligations, and both court women who will only marry a man called Ernest. Wilde’s 1895 comedy is probably the most perfect play in English. Handbags have never been the same.',
  'The Ballad of Reading Gaol':'Written after Wilde’s release from two years in prison, about a soldier hanged for killing his wife. “Each man kills the thing he loves.” It was first published as by C.3.3., his cell number.',
  'Intentions':'Wilde’s essays on art and criticism, written as witty dialogues. “The Decay of Lying” argues that life imitates art far more than art imitates life. Paradox is the method, and it is great fun.',
  "Lady Windermere's Fan":'A young wife suspects her husband of an affair with a woman of doubtful reputation, who turns out to be connected to her in ways she never imagined. Wilde’s first great success, full of lines people still quote.',
  'The Soul of Man under Socialism':'Wilde’s 1891 essay argues that socialism would free people to develop their individuality and make art. It is idealistic, provocative and very Wildean: “Disobedience, in the eyes of anyone who has read history, is man’s original virtue.”',
  // The Golden Age of Detection
  "Trent's Last Case":'A millionaire is found dead in his garden, and the painter-detective Philip Trent solves it brilliantly and completely wrongly. E. C. Bentley wrote it in 1913 as a parody of the infallible detective, and helped begin the Golden Age.',
  'The Red House Mystery':'Before Winnie-the-Pooh, A. A. Milne wrote this 1922 country-house mystery. A brother returns from Australia and is shot. An amateur sleuth and his friend investigate with great cheerfulness.',
  'The Mystery of the Yellow Room':'A young woman is attacked in a room locked from the inside, and the attacker vanishes. Gaston Leroux’s 1907 novel is one of the great locked-room puzzles, with a teenage reporter as detective.',
  'The Circular Staircase':'A spinster rents a country house for the summer, hears noises in the night and finds a body at the foot of the stairs. Mary Roberts Rinehart’s 1908 novel began the “Had-I-But-Known” school of mystery.',
  'The Old Man in the Corner':'An old man sits in a teashop tying knots in string and solves crimes that baffle the police, without ever leaving his chair. Baroness Orczy, creator of the Scarlet Pimpernel, invented one of the first armchair detectives.',
  'The Thirty-Nine Steps':'Richard Hannay finds a murdered spy in his flat and flees across the Scottish moors pursued by police and German agents. Buchan’s 1915 thriller invented the man-on-the-run story. It is short, fast and irresistible.',
  'The Leavenworth Case':'A wealthy New York merchant is shot in his library. Anna Katharine Green’s 1878 novel was a huge bestseller and one of the first detective novels by a woman. Her detective, Ebenezer Gryce, is a quiet, thoughtful professional.',
  'The Big Bow Mystery':'Israel Zangwill’s 1891 novel is the first full-length locked-room mystery. A man is found with his throat cut in a room bolted from inside, and the solution is famously ingenious.',
  // Firsts of Science
  'The Sidereal Messenger':'In 1610 Galileo turned a telescope on the sky and saw mountains on the Moon, countless new stars and four moons circling Jupiter. This short book announced it to the world, and the universe was never the same size again.',
  'Opticks':'Newton’s 1704 book on light: how white light splits into colours, how rainbows form, how lenses work. It ends with a series of “Queries” that range speculatively across physics and chemistry.',
  'The Chemical History of a Candle':'Michael Faraday’s Christmas lectures for children in 1860, explaining all of chemistry through a single burning candle. It is one of the finest pieces of science teaching ever given.',
  'Micrographia':'Robert Hooke’s 1665 book of microscope drawings revealed a gigantic flea, the eye of a fly and the “cells” of cork, a word he coined. Samuel Pepys stayed up until two in the morning reading it.',
  'Relativity: The Special and General Theory':'Einstein wrote this popular explanation of his theories in 1916 for readers without advanced mathematics. It is clear, patient and full of trains, lightning and falling elevators.',
  'Experiments and Observations on Electricity':'Benjamin Franklin’s letters on electricity, first published in London in 1751, proposed that lightning was electrical and described how to prove it. They made him famous in Europe as a scientist before he was known as a statesman.',
  'An Inquiry into the Causes and Effects of the Variolae Vaccinae':'In 1798 Edward Jenner, a country doctor, showed that infection with cowpox protected against smallpox. This short book described his experiments and introduced vaccination. Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980.',
  'Principles of Geology':'Charles Lyell argued that the Earth was shaped by slow processes still at work today, over immense time. Darwin took the first volume with him on the Beagle. It changed how people imagined the age of the world.',
  // Gardens
  'Elizabeth and Her German Garden':'An English wife on a Pomeranian estate escapes her husband (the Man of Wrath) and her duties to make a garden. Elizabeth von Arnim’s 1898 book is witty, rebellious and full of flowers.',
  'Wood and Garden':'Gertrude Jekyll, the great garden designer, describes her own garden at Munstead Wood through the year. Her eye for colour and planting changed English gardens. Her eyesight was poor, which makes her colour sense all the more remarkable.',
  "A Child's Garden of Verses":'Stevenson’s 1885 poems of childhood: bed in summer, the land of counterpane, my shadow. He was often ill as a child, and the poems remember a world made from imagination. Countless children have learned them by heart.',
  'The Wild Garden':'William Robinson argued in 1870 for gardens planted with hardy flowers allowed to grow naturally, instead of rigid Victorian bedding. His ideas shaped the cottage garden and much of modern gardening.',
  'My Summer in a Garden':'Charles Dudley Warner’s 1870 comic account of his vegetable garden in Hartford, Connecticut: weeds, birds, neighbours and borrowed advice. Mark Twain was a friend and neighbour, and it shows.',
  // Mountains
  'Scrambles Amongst the Alps in the Years 1860-69':'Edward Whymper’s account of his climbs, including the first ascent of the Matterhorn in 1865, which ended in the deaths of four of his companions on the descent. His own engravings illustrate it.',
  'A Tramp Abroad':'Twain’s 1880 account of a walking tour through Germany, Switzerland and Italy, in which he walks as little as possible. His ascent of the Riffelberg with a huge caravan of guides is gloriously absurd.',
  'Heidi':'An orphan girl is sent to live with her gruff grandfather on an Alpine mountainside, and loves it; taken to the city, she pines for home. Johanna Spyri’s 1881 novel made generations of readers long for the Alps.',
  'The Mountains of California':'John Muir’s first book, from 1894, about the Sierra Nevada: glaciers, forests, water ouzels and a windstorm he rode out at the top of a Douglas spruce. His writing helped create America’s national parks.',
  'My First Summer in the Sierra':'In 1869 John Muir worked as a shepherd in the Sierra Nevada and kept a journal of pure delight. Rocks, plants, bears and light all fill him with wonder. Every page glows.',
  'The Assault on Mount Everest, 1922':'The official account of the second British expedition to Everest, the first to attempt the summit. George Mallory was among the climbers, two years before he vanished near the top. They reached 27,000 feet without the modern equipment we take for granted.',
  'Hours of Exercise in the Alps':'John Tyndall, a physicist studying glaciers, was also a fine climber; he made the first ascent of the Weisshorn in 1861. His book combines science and mountaineering with real enthusiasm.',
  // The Secret Language of Flowers
  'Language of Flowers':'Kate Greenaway’s 1884 illustrated guide gives the meaning of each flower: daffodil for regard, lavender for distrust, yellow rose for jealousy. Her tiny, delicate pictures made it a treasured Victorian gift.',
  'The Language and Poetry of Flowers':'A Victorian gift book pairing each flower’s meaning with poems. Sweethearts could send whole conversations in bouquets, if both owned the same dictionary. They did not always.',
  'The Folk-Lore of Plants':'Thiselton-Dyer’s 1889 survey of the beliefs, superstitions and customs surrounding plants: witches’ herbs, lucky flowers, plants of love and death. It is a treasure trove.',
  'Plant Lore, Legends, and Lyrics':'Richard Folkard’s 1884 encyclopedia of myths and legends about plants from around the world, with poems and folklore. It is the kind of book you open to one page and leave three hours later.',
  // Hallowtide Folklore
  'The Golden Bough':'James Frazer’s vast study of magic and religion, beginning with a strange priesthood in a sacred grove near Rome. Many of his theories are now doubted, but it shaped modern literature. Its chapters on fire festivals and Hallowe’en are fascinating.',
  'Celtic Fairy Tales':'Joseph Jacobs gathered Irish, Scottish and Welsh tales in 1892: giants, fairy wives, magic horses and clever heroes. John D. Batten’s illustrations are wonderful. Read them aloud by the fire.',
  'The Fairy-Faith in Celtic Countries':'W. Y. Evans-Wentz travelled through Ireland, Scotland, Wales, Brittany and Cornwall around 1910 interviewing people who believed in fairies. Their testimony is extraordinary, and recorded with care.',
  'Fairy and Folk Tales of the Irish Peasantry':'W. B. Yeats edited this 1888 collection of Irish folk stories: the trooping fairies, changelings, ghosts, witches and the banshee. His notes show a young poet falling in love with his country’s folklore.',
  'Witchcraft & Second Sight in the Highlands & Islands of Scotland':'John Gregorson Campbell, a minister on the island of Tiree, collected beliefs about witches, charms and the second sight in the late nineteenth century. It records a world that was vanishing as he wrote.',
  'Poems and Songs of Robert Burns':'Burns’s poem “Halloween” describes the charms and games of a Scottish Halloween night in 1785: pulling kale stalks, burning nuts, eating an apple before a mirror to see a future husband. And there is “Tam o’ Shanter”, with its witches’ dance.',
  'Letters on Demonology and Witchcraft':'Walter Scott wrote these letters in 1830 to explain away belief in ghosts and witches. He does it by telling so many good ghost stories that the effect is not entirely reassuring.',
  'English Fairy Tales':'Joseph Jacobs’s 1890 collection includes Jack and the Beanstalk, the Three Little Pigs and Tom Tit Tot. He wanted English children to have their own fairy tales, as the Germans had the Grimms.',
  // Edgehill, 1642
  'Areopagitica':'Milton’s 1644 speech against censorship, written during the Civil War, argues for “the liberty to know, to utter, and to argue freely according to conscience, above all liberties.” It is still quoted in defences of free speech.',
  'Eikon Basilike':'Published days after Charles I’s execution in 1649, this book claimed to be the King’s own meditations in captivity. It was a huge success and helped create the image of Charles as a martyr. Milton wrote a furious reply.',
  'Lucasta':'Richard Lovelace was a royalist poet imprisoned for his loyalty. From prison he wrote “Stone walls do not a prison make, nor iron bars a cage.” These are the graceful poems of a cavalier.',
  'Woodstock':'Walter Scott’s 1826 novel is set in 1651, after the war, when the fugitive future Charles II hides at the royal lodge of Woodstock. Cromwell himself appears as a character.',
  'The Children of the New Forest':'Four royalist children, orphaned by the Civil War, hide in the New Forest and learn to live off the land with the help of a forester. Captain Marryat’s 1847 novel has been a children’s favourite ever since.',
  // Short and Funny
  'The Diary of a Nobody':'Mr Charles Pooter, a City clerk, records the tiny triumphs and many humiliations of his suburban life at The Laurels, Brickfield Terrace. The Grossmith brothers created an immortal bore. It is kind and funny at once.',
  'Nonsense Books':'Edward Lear’s limericks, nonsense songs and drawings: the Owl and the Pussy-cat, the Jumblies who went to sea in a sieve, the Dong with a luminous nose. Pure delight, and in places, surprisingly melancholy.',
  'Literary Lapses':'Stephen Leacock’s 1910 collection of comic sketches, including “My Financial Career”, in which a nervous man tries to open a bank account. Leacock was a professor of economics and the funniest man in Canada.',
  'Nonsense Novels':'Leacock’s 1911 parodies of popular fiction: detective stories, sea tales and romances. Lord Ronald “flung himself upon his horse and rode madly off in all directions” comes from here.',
  'Mr. Dooley in Peace and in War':'Finley Peter Dunne’s Irish-American bartender comments on politics and the Spanish–American War to his customer Mr Hennessy, in thick Chicago-Irish dialect. The satire is sharp enough to have worried presidents.',
  'The Idle Thoughts of an Idle Fellow':'Jerome K. Jerome’s 1886 essays on being idle, being hard up, being shy, the weather and cats. Its success made his career, three years before Three Men in a Boat. Gentle, comic and very readable.',
  "The Devil's Dictionary":'Ambrose Bierce’s cynical definitions: “Bore, n. A person who talks when you wish him to listen.” “Positive, adj. Mistaken at the top of one’s voice.” Keep it by the bed, and read one or two at a time.',
  'How to Tell a Story and Other Essays':'Twain explains the art of the humorous story, and the difference between American, English and French jokes, with a demonstration: the ghost story “The Golden Arm”, which ends with a shout.',
  // Russian Classics
  'Fathers and Children':'Turgenev’s 1862 novel about a young nihilist, Bazarov, who rejects all authority and tradition, and clashes with his friend’s father and uncle. It caused a storm in Russia, as both old and young thought themselves insulted.',
  'Dead Souls':'The swindler Chichikov travels rural Russia buying up the names of dead serfs, who still count as property on paper. Gogol’s 1842 comic novel gives a gallery of grotesque landowners. He burned the second part.',
  'A Hero of Our Time':'Pechorin is a bored, brilliant, cruel young officer in the Caucasus. Lermontov’s 1840 novel is told in linked stories and journals, and Pechorin is the first great anti-hero of Russian literature. Lermontov was killed in a duel at twenty-six.',
  'Oblomov':'Ilya Ilyich Oblomov is so lazy that it takes him fifty pages to get out of bed. Goncharov’s 1859 novel is comic and melancholy, and Russians still use “Oblomovism” to describe his particular inertia.',
  'The Queen of Spades':'A young officer obsessed with a secret of three winning cards frightens an old countess to death to learn it. Pushkin’s 1834 story is a perfect gothic tale of greed and madness. Tchaikovsky made it an opera.',
  'The Lady with the Dog and Other Stories':'In Yalta, a married man begins an affair with a young woman walking a little dog, and to his surprise it becomes real love. Chekhov’s story is quiet and profound, and the ending is left open.',
  // Firsts for Children
  'Orbis Pictus':'John Amos Comenius’s 1658 “Visible World in Pictures” taught Latin through labelled woodcuts of everything from God to the barber’s shop. It is often called the first picture book for children.',
  'The Water-Babies':'Tom, a young chimney sweep, falls into a river and becomes a water-baby. Charles Kingsley’s 1863 fairy tale mixes adventure, satire, science and moral lessons. It helped change laws on child labour.',
  "Tom Brown's School Days":'Thomas Hughes’s 1857 novel of life at Rugby School under Dr Arnold, with its bullying, fighting and friendship. It invented the school story, and its bully Flashman later got his own series of novels by another author.',
  'Struwwelpeter':'Heinrich Hoffmann wrote these cautionary tales in 1845 for his son: the boy who wouldn’t cut his nails, the girl who played with matches, the thumb-sucker. They are gruesome and were meant to be funny.',
  'The Real Mother Goose':'The classic 1916 American collection of nursery rhymes, with Blanche Fisher Wright’s bright illustrations. Humpty Dumpty, Little Bo-Peep and Old King Cole, the oldest poems most of us ever learned.',
  'The Swiss Family Robinson':'A Swiss pastor, his wife and four sons are shipwrecked on a tropical island and build a remarkable home, including a treehouse. The animal list is impossible, and the adventure is irresistible.',
  // Friendship
  'Treatises on Friendship and Old Age':'Cicero wrote these two dialogues in 44 BC, the year Caesar was murdered. On Friendship insists that true friendship exists only between good people and is life’s greatest gift. On Old Age is a consolation still worth reading.',
  'Essays of Michel de Montaigne — Complete':'Montaigne invented the essay: “Of Friendship” remembers Étienne de La Boétie, the friend he lost, and explains their bond as “because it was he, because it was I.” Every essay is a conversation with an honest, curious mind.',
  'The Story of My Life':'Helen Keller, deaf and blind from infancy, describes the day her teacher Anne Sullivan spelled “water” into her hand and the world opened. She wrote this in 1903, while still a student at Radcliffe.',
  // The Moon and Beyond
  'A Journey in Other Worlds':'John Jacob Astor IV, one of the richest men in the world, wrote this 1894 novel of the year 2000, with trips to Jupiter and Saturn by antigravity. He died on the Titanic in 1912.',
  'A Voyage to the Moon':'Cyrano de Bergerac, the real seventeenth-century writer later made famous by Rostand’s play, describes several ingenious methods of reaching the Moon, including bottles of dew. It is satire, philosophy and fantasy at once.',
  "Edison's Conquest of Mars":'An unofficial 1898 sequel to The War of the Worlds, in which Thomas Edison invents weapons and leads a fleet to Mars. Garrett Serviss was an astronomer, and it is gleefully patriotic.',
  'Across the Zodiac':'Percy Greg’s 1880 novel sends a spaceship powered by anti-gravity to Mars, where the narrator finds a strange society. It is one of the earliest novels of interplanetary travel.',
  'Lumen':'Camille Flammarion, a French astronomer, imagined a soul travelling through space and looking back at light from Earth’s past. His 1872 dialogues are scientific speculation and cosmic reverie.',
  // Mediums, Magicians and Hoaxes
  'A Magician Among the Spirits':'Houdini’s 1924 exposé of fraudulent mediums, describing the tricks they used at séances. He had once been friends with Arthur Conan Doyle, a firm believer, and their friendship broke over this subject.',
  'The Vital Message':'Arthur Conan Doyle lost his son and his brother at the end of the First World War, and turned to spiritualism. In this 1919 book he argues for life after death. It is sincere and sad, and a long way from Sherlock Holmes.',
  'The Coming of the Fairies':'In 1917 two girls in Cottingley, Yorkshire, photographed fairies. Conan Doyle was convinced they were real and wrote this book in 1922 to prove it. Decades later the girls admitted they had used paper cut-outs.',
  'Modern Magic':'Professor Hoffmann (Angelo Lewis, a barrister) published this 1876 guide to conjuring tricks. It became the bible of stage magicians for generations. Coins, cards, handkerchiefs: every secret revealed.',
  'The Moon Hoax':'In 1835 the New York Sun reported that the astronomer John Herschel had discovered bat-winged people living on the Moon. Many readers believed it. It remains one of the great newspaper hoaxes.',
  'The Discoverie of Witchcraft':'Reginald Scot argued in 1584 that witchcraft was an illusion and that accused witches were poor, harmless women. He explained conjurers’ tricks to prove his point. King James I ordered the book burned.',
  // Keats, Born Today
  'Poems Published in 1820':'Keats’s last book, published months before his death: the great odes (to a Nightingale, on a Grecian Urn, to Autumn), “The Eve of St. Agnes”, “Lamia” and “Hyperion”. Perhaps the finest single book of poems in English.',
  'Endymion':'Keats’s long 1818 poem about a shepherd who loves the moon goddess begins “A thing of beauty is a joy for ever.” The reviewers attacked it savagely. He knew its faults better than they did.',
  'Letters of John Keats to Fanny Brawne':'The love letters of Keats to his neighbour Fanny Brawne, written in the last years of his life while he was ill and poor. They are passionate, jealous and heartbreaking. Their publication in 1878 caused a scandal.',
  'Adonais':'Shelley’s elegy for Keats, written in 1821 after hearing of his death. He blamed the harsh reviewers for killing Keats, and the poem rises to a vision of the dead poet made one with nature. Shelley drowned the next year.',
  'Keats':'Sidney Colvin’s short 1887 biography of Keats for the English Men of Letters series is clear and sympathetic, and still a good introduction.',
  'Letters of John Keats to His Family and Friends':'Keats’s letters are among the best in English: full of thoughts on poetry (“negative capability”, “the vale of soul-making”), jokes, and love for his brothers and sister. They show a brilliant mind at work.',
  'Poems 1817':'Keats’s first book, published when he was twenty-one. It includes “On First Looking into Chapman’s Homer”, the sonnet about reading Homer for the first time, which is itself a first look at a great poet.',
  // Essays for a Quiet Morning
  'Table-Talk':'William Hazlitt’s essays of the 1820s: “On Going a Journey”, “On the Pleasure of Painting”, “On the Ignorance of the Learned”. They are vigorous, opinionated and a pleasure to argue with.',
  'Walking':'Thoreau’s essay on the art of walking, published just after his death in 1862. “In Wildness is the preservation of the World.” It is short enough to read before a walk, and good company on one.',
  'The Pleasures of Life':'John Lubbock, a banker, scientist and politician, wrote this 1887 book on the joys of books, friends, work, nature and travel. It was hugely popular, and his list of the hundred best books started a craze.',
  'Sesame and Lilies':'John Ruskin’s lectures of 1865 on reading, on the treasures of books (“kings’ treasuries”) and on the role of women. Parts are dated; his passion for serious reading is not.',
  'Virginibus Puerisque, and Other Papers':'Robert Louis Stevenson’s essays on marriage, youth, idleness and walking tours, including “An Apology for Idlers”. They are charming, clever and full of the joy of life.',
  // Early Feminist Voices
  'The Subjection of Women':'John Stuart Mill argued in 1869 that the legal subordination of women was wrong and a hindrance to human progress. He wrote it with the help of his wife, Harriet Taylor Mill, and daughter Helen Taylor.',
  'Woman in the Nineteenth Century':'Margaret Fuller, editor of the Transcendentalist magazine The Dial, argued in 1845 for women’s equality in every sphere. It was a founding text of American feminism. She died in a shipwreck five years later.',
  'Women and Economics':'Charlotte Perkins Gilman argued in 1898 that women’s economic dependence on men was harmful to everyone, and imagined kitchenless houses and shared childcare. It was translated into many languages.',
  'Incidents in the Life of a Slave Girl':'Harriet Jacobs escaped slavery by hiding for seven years in a tiny crawlspace in her grandmother’s house. Her 1861 autobiography, published under a pseudonym, tells of the particular sexual abuse faced by enslaved women.',
  'My Own Story':'Emmeline Pankhurst, founder of the Women’s Social and Political Union, told the story of the suffragette campaign in 1914: marches, hunger strikes, force-feeding and prison. “Deeds, not words.”',
  'Eighty Years and More':'Elizabeth Cady Stanton, who helped organise the 1848 Seneca Falls convention on women’s rights, wrote this memoir in 1898. It is lively, funny and full of the great reform campaigns of her century.',
  // First to the Ends of the Earth
  'The South Pole':'Roald Amundsen’s own account of how his Norwegian party reached the South Pole in December 1911, a month before Scott. His careful planning, skis and dogs made the difference. It is calm, practical and gripping.',
  'The Worst Journey in the World':'Apsley Cherry-Garrard was one of the youngest members of Scott’s last expedition. His 1922 account of a winter journey to collect emperor penguin eggs, and of finding Scott’s tent, is one of the greatest books of exploration.',
  'Farthest North':'Fridtjof Nansen let his ship Fram freeze into the Arctic ice and drift across the polar sea, then set out on skis for the North Pole. He reached the farthest north yet in 1895, and survived a winter in a stone hut.',
  'The North Pole':'Robert Peary’s 1910 account of his 1909 expedition to the North Pole, with Matthew Henson and four Inuit men. Whether they actually reached the Pole is still debated.',
  'Travels in the Interior of Africa':'The Scottish explorer Mungo Park set out in 1795 to find the course of the Niger. His account of the journey, of his captivity and illness, and of the people who helped him, was a bestseller. He died on a second expedition.',
  'The Journals of Lewis and Clark':'From 1804 to 1806 Meriwether Lewis and William Clark led the Corps of Discovery from St. Louis to the Pacific and back. Their journals record rivers, plants, animals and the peoples they met, spelled with glorious inventiveness.',
  'A Voyage Towards the South Pole and Round the World':'Captain James Cook’s account of his second voyage of 1772–75, when he crossed the Antarctic Circle and proved there was no great southern continent in temperate latitudes. He came closer to Antarctica than anyone before him.',
  'Personal Narrative of a Pilgrimage to Al-Madinah and Meccah':'In 1853 Richard Burton travelled to Mecca disguised as a Muslim pilgrim, a dangerous journey for a European. His account is detailed, adventurous and full of his own restless curiosity.',
};
